\section{Introduction}

This document summarizes the discussion on the relationship between the infinite-dimensional complex Clifford algebra \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\) and the CAR algebra (Canonical Anticommutation Relations algebra). We also explore the construction of the direct limit of algebras and its role in defining \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\).

\section{Infinite-Dimensional Clifford Algebra}

The infinite-dimensional Clifford algebra \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\) is generated by an infinite sequence of generators \(\{e_i\}_{i=1}^\infty\) satisfying the anticommutation relations:
\[
e_i e_j + e_j e_i = 2 \delta_{ij},
\]
where \(\delta_{ij}\) is the Kronecker delta. This algebra can be constructed as the direct limit of finite-dimensional Clifford algebras \(\text{Cl}(2n, \mathbb{C})\) as \(n \to \infty\).

\section{Direct Limit of Algebras}

The direct limit (or inductive limit) of a directed system of algebras is a construction that generalizes the idea of taking a "limit" of a sequence of algebraic structures. For the Clifford algebras, the directed system is:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A directed set \(I = \mathbb{N}\), ordered by \(n \leq m\).
    \item For each \(n \in \mathbb{N}\), an algebra \(A_n = \text{Cl}(2n, \mathbb{C})\).
    \item For each pair \(n \leq m\), a homomorphism \(\phi_{nm}: \text{Cl}(2n, \mathbb{C}) \to \text{Cl}(2m, \mathbb{C})\), which is the natural inclusion map.
\end{itemize}

The direct limit \(\varinjlim \text{Cl}(2n, \mathbb{C})\) is the infinite-dimensional Clifford algebra \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\). It consists of equivalence classes of elements from the finite-dimensional Clifford algebras, where two elements are equivalent if they become equal after inclusion into a larger Clifford algebra.

\section{CAR Algebra}

The CAR algebra is a \(C^*\)-algebra generated by creation and annihilation operators \(\{a_i, a_i^*\}_{i=1}^\infty\) satisfying the canonical anticommutation relations:
\[
\{a_i, a_j\} = 0, \quad \{a_i, a_j^*\} = \delta_{ij}, \quad \{a_i^*, a_j^*\} = 0.
\]
These relations describe the algebraic structure of fermionic systems in quantum mechanics and quantum field theory.

\section{Isomorphism Between \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\) and the CAR Algebra}

The infinite-dimensional Clifford algebra \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\) is isomorphic to the CAR algebra. This isomorphism arises because the generators of \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\) and the CAR algebra satisfy similar algebraic relations. Explicitly:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The generators \(e_i\) of \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\) satisfy \(e_i e_j + e_j e_i = 2 \delta_{ij}\).
    \item The creation and annihilation operators \(a_i, a_i^*\) of the CAR algebra satisfy \(\{a_i, a_j\} = 0\) and \(\{a_i, a_j^*\} = \delta_{ij}\).
\end{itemize}

The correspondence can be made explicit by defining:
\[
a_i = \frac{e_i + i e_{i+1}}{2}, \quad a_i^* = \frac{e_i - i e_{i+1}}{2}.
\]
These operators satisfy the CAR relations, and the algebra generated by \(\{a_i, a_i^*\}\) is isomorphic to the CAR algebra.

\section{Conclusion}

The infinite-dimensional complex Clifford algebra \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\) is isomorphic to the CAR algebra. Both algebras describe the same underlying algebraic structure, with the generators of \(\text{Cl}(\infty, \mathbb{C})\) corresponding to the creation and annihilation operators of the CAR algebra. This isomorphism is a fundamental result in the study of fermionic systems and operator algebras.

